00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:51.680
Somewhere around 470 million years ago,&nbsp;
something happened that shouldn’t have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:58.320
been particularly striking. An&nbsp;
algae found its way onto land.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:06.720
On its own, that’s not an unusual event. Over&nbsp;
billions of years, many different types of algae&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:12.720
have been able to find their way from&nbsp;
their freshwater or marine homes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:17.680
and carve out new lives in&nbsp;
niches they found on land.

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:24.000
But something was different about this algae,

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:29.520
something special. It would take&nbsp;
the algae on an evolutionary journey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:35.795
through millions and millions of years,&nbsp;
changing our whole world in the process.

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:42.960
This algae turned the lands of this earth green,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:50.000
altered the chemistry of our atmosphere,&nbsp;
and created homes for future life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:57.203
This algae would give rise to all&nbsp;
of the land plants we know of today.

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:06.240
Land plants are incredibly diverse. They’ve&nbsp;
had all those hundreds of millions of years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:14.400
to get that way. Some have trunks, some&nbsp;
have flowers, some are low to the ground.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:21.840
Even in a small patch of forest,&nbsp;
the variations can seem endless.

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:28.560
But all of those plants belong to a&nbsp;
single clade called the Embryophyta.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:35.360
They’re divided into two broad&nbsp;
categories. There are the bryophytes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:41.360
which lack the vasculature to grow tall.&nbsp;
These include mosses and liverworts.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:48.800
And then there are the tracheophytes, which&nbsp;
do have vascular tissues that carry water&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:54.320
and support their growth. These are,&nbsp;
well, just about everything else.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:59.920
Trees, flowers, the plant climbing&nbsp;
up the corner of your room.

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:06.000
All land plants are descended&nbsp;
from an ancient member of a group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:13.360
that’s still around called the Streptophyte&nbsp;
algae. But what was so special about this algae?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:21.760
What enabled it to cultivate lands and take them&nbsp;
over to an extent that no other algae could?

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:30.720
We don’t know. It’s a fundamental mystery&nbsp;
of plants, because their existence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:32.880
is just that remarkable.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:43.520
Land is a stressful place to settle down on,&nbsp;
especially for an algae that is making its way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:50.960
out of water. It would have had to contend with&nbsp;
so much more sun than before, unfiltered and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:58.240
unrefracted by water. And that would put the&nbsp;
algae at risk for damage to its cell and its DNA.

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:05.200
On top of that, land is dry. It’s&nbsp;
easy to take water for granted when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:13.040
you live in a pond or an ocean. But land makes&nbsp;
you even more dependent on the weather to provide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:18.240
moisture, and on the morsels of water&nbsp;
you can gather from your environment.&nbsp;

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:26.080
So whatever algae made its way onto land, it&nbsp;
had to have contained incredible potential,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:30.640
an enormous capacity to contend with the stresses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:36.160
of an environment that was fundamentally&nbsp;
different from the home it was leaving.

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:44.480
While we don’t know exactly what that potential&nbsp;
looked like, scientists do have some theories.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:52.560
One idea is that this mysterious ancestral&nbsp;
algae may have been able to cheat a little…

00:04:53.600 --> 00:05:01.200
that&nbsp;it was somewhat predisposed to life on land&nbsp;
because maybe it originally lived in freshwater,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:08.080
where conditions are a little closer&nbsp;
to land compared to marine habitats.

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:17.120
It could also be that the streptophyte algae just&nbsp;
got here first. That maybe it beat other algae to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:24.160
the punch, and that because it was first, it&nbsp;
had very little competition as it adapted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:32.560
the new challenges it was facing. It could thrive&nbsp;
without having to struggle for space or resources,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:37.280
taking over the land and developing new structures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:43.440
that would give it an advantage over any other&nbsp;
algae that tried to come onto land later.

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:50.880
Those are a few general theories about what&nbsp;
might have enabled the streptophyte algae to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:59.760
adapt readily to land. But scientists also wonder&nbsp;
about the specifics—the details of the algae’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:06.560
appearance that would have morphed into the&nbsp;
traits that allowed embryophytes to dominate land.

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:14.400
And with the genetic tools available today,&nbsp;
scientists have made progress on understanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:21.360
more of this mysterious past. They’ve identified&nbsp;
the closest living relatives to land plants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:28.640
a group of streptophyte algae called&nbsp;
Zygnematales that includes this spirogyra. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:35.440
scientists have used this relationship to form&nbsp;
new hypotheses around ancient family traits.

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:42.800
Plus, there are many, many, many plants&nbsp;
in the world whose present-day forms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:46.640
reveal the messy web that evolution has spun.

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:55.680
Take, for example, the stomata. You can see them&nbsp;
here, the microscopic openings that help the plant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.680 --> 00:07:02.640
take in carbon dioxide and release water.&nbsp;
The stomata is the kind of adaptation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:10.400
that is key to plant survival on land, creating&nbsp;
many tiny valves that tune the plant’s interior&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:17.760
to best match whatever the changing world&nbsp;
around it demands. Scientists have even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:24.720
found stomata-like openings on plant fossils&nbsp;
dating back more than 400 million years ago.

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:33.120
Not all modern day plants have stomata though,&nbsp;
including some bryophytes like liverworts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:41.920
So scientists had to dive into the phylogenetic&nbsp;
history woven into plant DNA to figure out that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:50.160
the first land plants likely did have stomata,&nbsp;
and that bryophytes may have reduced their stomata&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:55.520
over time to the point that some&nbsp;
even lost their stomata entirely.

00:07:56.080 --> 00:08:04.480
It’s all a bit like looking at your family members&nbsp;
today to try and figure out whether an ancestor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:13.840
millions of years ago had dimples. The results&nbsp;
are a clue into one small feature of an ancient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:23.360
algae that made its way onto land, a sign that it&nbsp;
may have had its own ancestral form of a stomata.

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:31.280
And if that’s the case, then perhaps an&nbsp;
ancient bryophyte inherited that stomata&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:40.240
only to eventually lose it through many cycles of&nbsp;
change and selection. It would hardly be the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:47.920
plant to take its inheritance from that remarkable&nbsp;
algae and then decide to do away with it.

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:55.360
These cells belong to Egeria densa. You&nbsp;
may have seen it before. It’s a plant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.360 --> 00:09:03.120
commonly found in aquariums. And yet this is&nbsp;
an embryophyte, a member of the land plants.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:07.840
It just happened to have found&nbsp;
its way back into the water,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:13.280
leaving behind the land that its&nbsp;
unknown ancestor once conquered.

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:19.440
It can be difficult to piece&nbsp;
together why anything in evolution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:28.080
happens the way it happens. It’s easy, with&nbsp;
hundreds of millions of years of hindsight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:33.120
to describe these events with&nbsp;
some sort of inevitability.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:42.560
After all, we live in a world covered in plants.&nbsp;
We live because of a world covered in plants.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:51.600
To conceive of our world without plants would be&nbsp;
to conceive of a world without us as we are today.

00:09:52.400 --> 00:10:01.200
But maybe the point of all this is that hindsight&nbsp;
is a convenient story we tell ourselves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:04.480
and that none of this was inevitable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:13.720
An algae came onto land, and it changed the&nbsp;
world. The rest, as they say, is history.

